{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790923965,"nonce":"tkXiluHOB4p9+iDoBlyfBpxoezw=","title":"No page tells an operator when an incident tool needs a bridge instead of a payload mapping, or warns that resolving on quiet ends a response the moment the signals stop","message":"**What is wrong.** ADR 0055 makes two promises to operators that only documentation can keep. §3: \"OAuth flows, several calls per fact, a vendor's REST API for notes: a small service the operator runs, which receives oto's webhook. It runs out of oto's process, in any language. oto documents the pattern; it does not host bridges.\" §4: \"The docs say plainly that `quiet` is not `fixed`, and that a mapping which resolves on `quiet` ends a response the moment the signals stop.\" Neither is written anywhere: `docs/setup/` holds `configuration.md`, `slack.md`, `slack-live-verification.md`, `tuning.md` and `victoriametrics.md`, and no page outside `docs/adr/` mentions a bridge or the meaning of `quiet` to a receiver.\n\n**The demonstration.** An operator who wants PagerDuty incidents to resolve when oto's Incident goes quiet can do it in one line of an operator-written mapping (§4 lets them), and an operator who wants incident.io notes per Finding cannot do it with a mapping at all (one call per fact, REST API). Today the docs answer neither: the first operator is not told what they are giving up, and the second is not told that a bridge is the sanctioned shape — so the obvious next request is \"add a PagerDuty provider to oto\", which ADR 0052 §5 and ADR 0055 §5 have both refused.\n\n**Why it matters.** `quiet` means every member Case is currently closed (`internal/channels/render/webhookjson/envelope.go`, `Incident.State`: \"`active` while any current member Case is open and `quiet` otherwise. Derived by oto from its Cases and never set by anyone\"). A flapping alert, a scrape outage or a rule edit all produce `quiet` with nothing fixed. A receiver that resolves on it closes the incident, stops the escalation and pages nobody when the signal returns as `active_again`. ADR 0055 put that choice in the operator's hands on the condition that the docs say what it costs.\n\n**Where.** A new `docs/setup/incident-tools.md` (synced into the docs site; `setup/` is autogenerated in the sidebar), linked from the webhook page 2765f74 creates. It covers, in order: the three ways to reach an incident tool — the plain `oto.notification.v1` envelope (for a tool that accepts arbitrary JSON and maps fields itself), a payload mapping on the webhook Connection (2205620, with the catalog from 2b5eecc), or a bridge; how to choose (a mapping when one request per fact in a fixed shape suffices; a bridge for OAuth, several calls per fact, reading a response beyond `external_url`/`external_id`, or anything with logic); the bridge pattern itself — receive the webhook, verify `X-Oto-Signature` per the webhook page, de-duplicate on `X-Oto-Delivery-Id` (the queue is at-least-once, `internal/channels/providers/webhook/channel.go:115-119`), key the external incident on `incident.id`, return 2xx only once the work is durable since a non-2xx is retried or marked dead per its status (`channel.go:200-237`), and optionally echo `external_url`/`external_id` (506ff21); a minimal bridge sketch in prose and one short code listing, explicitly unsupported and not shipped; and a call-out, in ADR 0055 §4's words, that `quiet` is not `fixed` and what a resolve-on-quiet mapping or bridge ends, with `active_again` named as the fact that will arrive afterwards to an already-closed incident.\n\n**Scope.** small.\n\n**Done when.** `docs/setup/incident-tools.md` exists and renders in the docs site; it states the three routes and the rule for choosing; it describes the bridge pattern with signature verification, delivery-id de-duplication, `incident.id` keying and the 2xx/retry contract, each matching the code it cites; the `quiet`-is-not-`fixed` call-out is present and quotes §4; it says plainly that oto ships no vendor provider and hosts no bridge (ADR 0055 §§3, 5); `just ci` is green (`lint-vocabulary` included).\n\n**Blocked by.** 2765f74 (the signature and envelope contract this page links to), 2205620 (the mapping it describes).\n\n**Mode.** AFK. Source: ADR 0055 §§3–4, ticket-filing session 2026-10-02.","files":null}]}